\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate the CRM pipeline on two LIBERO benchmarks, asking: (i)~can adaptive compute allocation match fixed-$K$ performance at a fraction of the cost? (ii)~what drives the improvement, selection or correction? (iii)~how does conditioning interact with correction magnitude? (iv)~where does the pipeline fail?

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\paragraph{Benchmarks and base VLA.}
We use LIBERO-Object (10 tasks, novel object instances) and LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks, spatial reasoning)~\cite{liu2023libero}. Each task is evaluated over 10 rollouts (max 300 steps); success is binary.
We build on SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M parameters, flow matching), using dual-camera RGB ($256{\times}256$) and 8D proprioceptive state. Actions are 7D (6D pose + 1D gripper); the gripper is thresholded outside the pipeline.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
All methods use the same frozen SmolVLA: \textbf{VLA-only} ($K{=}1$, no correction), \textbf{Random} ($K{=}5{/}10$, uniform sample, no CRM), and \textbf{Argmin} ($K{=}5{/}10$, $L_2$ selection, no CRM).

\paragraph{Training and statistics.}
The CRM is trained on 26{,}737 samples (10 demos $\times$ 20 tasks, $K{=}10$ forward passes each), Adam $\mathrm{lr}{=}10^{-3}$, batch 32, 50 epochs, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$. All evaluation tasks are covered by CRM training data (in-distribution).
We report seed-level Mann-Whitney U tests (two-sided); 3--7 seeds per condition, none discarded.

\subsection{Adaptive Inference-Time Compute}
\label{sec:adaptive_k}

Fixed $K$ wastes compute: most timesteps produce low-variance action samples that need no correction, while a minority exhibit high uncertainty where the CRM provides critical gains (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}). An effective adaptive policy requires an uncertainty signal that is both cheap (available at $K{=}2$) and predictive of full-$K$ correction benefit.

\paragraph{Signal validation.}
The $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distance in action space correlates near-perfectly with $K{=}10$ sample variance ($\rho{=}0.9979$; Figure~\ref{fig:k2_correlation}). This correlation validates the two-sample probe as a reliable uncertainty estimate for deciding whether to escalate to the full pipeline.

Three alternative signals fail. Flow-matching velocity divergence (FMVD), computed in the model's latent space, yields $\rho{=}0.08$: latent-space disagreement does not translate to action-space uncertainty. CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}1$ ($\rho{=}0.15$) and $K{=}2$ ($\rho{=}0.13$) are equally uninformative, because the CRM's output magnitude reflects learned bias rather than input uncertainty. Only the raw action-space distance provides a viable signal.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig5_k2_correlation.pdf}
\caption{$K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distance vs.\ $K{=}10$ sample variance across all evaluation timesteps ($\rho{=}0.9979$). The two-sample distance is a near-perfect proxy for full-sample uncertainty, enabling cheap adaptive $K$ allocation.}
\label{fig:k2_correlation}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Threshold sweep.}
We set a percentile threshold $\tau$ on the $K{=}2$ $L_2$ distribution: timesteps exceeding $\tau$ escalate to $K{=}10$ with CRM correction, while the rest use the $K{=}2$ argmin action directly. Table~\ref{tab:pareto} reports the Pareto frontier on LIBERO-Object.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Adaptive $K$ threshold sweep on LIBERO-Object. $\tau$: percentile of the $K{=}2$ $L_2$ distribution used as the escalation threshold. \emph{trigger}: fraction of timesteps escalated to $K{=}10$. $\Delta$: relative SR change vs.\ fixed $K{=}10$.}
\label{tab:pareto}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
$\tau$ & SR (\%) & avg\_$K$ & trigger & $\Delta$ vs.\ $K{=}10$ \\
\midrule
P25 & {[TBD]} & {[TBD]} & {[TBD]} & --- \\
\textbf{P50} & $\mathbf{14.6}$ & $\mathbf{3.8}$ & $\mathbf{23\%}$ & $\mathbf{-13\%}$ \\
P75 & {[TBD]} & {[TBD]} & {[TBD]} & --- \\
P90 & {[TBD]} & {[TBD]} & {[TBD]} & --- \\
\midrule
Fixed $K{=}10$ & $16.8$ & $10.0$ & $100\%$ & ref \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The P50 threshold achieves 87\% of fixed-$K{=}10$ performance ($14.6$\% vs.\ $16.8$\% SR) at 38\% of the compute budget (avg\_$K{=}3.8$). 77\% of timesteps take the fast path ($K{=}2$, no CRM), and only 23\% escalate to the full pipeline. The CRM's correction benefit concentrates in a small fraction of high-uncertainty timesteps; blanket application of the full pipeline is unnecessary for the majority of the action distribution.

\paragraph{Pareto dominance.}
P50 Pareto-dominates P25: it achieves both higher SR and lower average $K$. The P25 threshold triggers full correction on timesteps where the VLA's samples already agree. On these low-uncertainty timesteps, the CRM applies corrections that are unnecessary and occasionally harmful, both wasting compute and reducing task success. Selective escalation outperforms aggressive escalation precisely because the CRM's value is concentrated at high-uncertainty decision points.

\paragraph{Connection to correction mechanism.}
The decomposition in Section~\ref{sec:main_results} shows that at $K{=}10$, correction accounts for 82\% of the total gain ($+$4.5\,pp of $+$5.5\,pp). The adaptive policy exploits this concentration: rather than applying correction uniformly, it targets timesteps where action-space disagreement signals genuine need for refinement. The $K{=}1$ failure mode (10.3\% $<$ baseline; Section~\ref{sec:main_results}) confirms that correction without adequate sample diversity is harmful, which justifies the two-stage design: probe uncertainty first, correct only when warranted.

\subsection{Main Results and Component Decomposition}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} compares all methods on both benchmarks.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Task success rate (\%\,$\uparrow$) on LIBERO-Object and LIBERO-Spatial. Mean $\pm$ std across $n$ seeds; 10 tasks $\times$ 10 rollouts per seed. CRM trained on all 20 LIBERO tasks (in-distribution). $\Delta$: vs.\ VLA-only.}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & $K$ & Object (\%) & $n$ & Spatial (\%) & $\Delta$ Obj \\
\midrule
VLA-only & 1 & $11.3 \pm 1.2$ & 6 & $29.3 \pm 1.7$ & --- \\
Random & 5 & $10.5 \pm 2.9$ & 4 & --- & $-0.8$ \\
Argmin & 5 & $13.0 \pm 1.6$ & 4 & --- & $+1.7$ \\
\textbf{CRM obs\_only} & \textbf{5} & $\mathbf{16.0 \pm 2.2}$ & \textbf{7} & $\mathbf{34.5 \pm 2.1}$ & $\mathbf{+4.7}$ \\
\midrule
Random & 10 & $11.7 \pm 2.3$ & 3 & --- & $+0.4$ \\
Argmin & 10 & $12.3 \pm 2.6$ & 6 & --- & $+1.0$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 10 & $16.8 \pm 1.8$ & 6 & $33.9 \pm 1.4$ & $+5.5$ \\
\midrule
CRM perdof & 10 & $11.0 \pm 1.8$ & 6 & --- & $-0.3$ \\
CRM scalar & 10 & $\phantom{0}8.5 \pm 1.5$ & 4 & --- & $-2.8$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Cross-benchmark improvement.}
CRM obs\_only at $K{=}10$ reaches $16.8 \pm 1.8$\% on Object (6 seeds, $p{=}0.003$ vs.\ VLA-only) and $33.9 \pm 1.4$\% on Spatial: $+$5.5\,pp and $+$4.6\,pp respectively. At $K{=}5$, the pipeline captures ${\sim}$85\% of the $K{=}10$ benefit ($+$4.7\,pp vs.\ $+$5.5\,pp on Object) at half the sample budget.

\paragraph{Decomposition at $K{=}10$.}
Random selection ($11.7$\%) is indistinguishable from VLA-only ($11.3$\%), and argmin adds only $+$0.6\,pp. CRM correction contributes the remaining $+$4.5\,pp, accounting for 82\% of the total $+$5.5\,pp gain. Correction is the sole active mechanism: at $K{=}10$ the $L_2$-nearest sample is already close to the centroid, so selection adds negligible value.

\paragraph{Decomposition at $K{=}5$.}
The pattern shifts (Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). Selection contributes $+$2.5\,pp (argmin $13.0$\% vs.\ random $10.5$\%, 45\% of the total gain) and correction adds $+$3.0\,pp (CRM $16.0$\% vs.\ argmin $13.0$\%, 55\%). Both mechanisms are active because fewer samples make the argmin selection step more informative.

\paragraph{$K{=}1$ failure.}
At $K{=}1$, correction without centering falls below baseline ($10.3$\% vs.\ $11.3$\%): the CRM receives an arbitrary single sample rather than the argmin-centered input it was trained on. This failure validates the adaptive policy (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k}), which always maintains at least $K{=}2$ to preserve the centering property.

\subsection{Conditioning-Magnitude Interaction}
\label{sec:magnitude_results}

Figure~\ref{fig:magnitude} plots success rate against the magnitude bound $\varepsilon$ on LIBERO-Object.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig2_magnitude.pdf}
\caption{Success rate vs.\ magnitude bound $\varepsilon$ on LIBERO-Object. obs\_only remains stable across all $\varepsilon$; perdof collapses to 0\% as the bound is relaxed. Error bars: $\pm$1 std.}
\label{fig:magnitude}
\end{figure}

obs\_only maintains 14.3--17.6\% across $\varepsilon \in [0.03, \text{uncapped}]$, with peak performance at $\varepsilon{=}0.2$. perdof collapses from 14.0\% ($\varepsilon{=}0.03$) to 0\% when uncapped, because relaxing the bound amplifies noisy correction directions from rank-deficient covariance estimates ($K{=}10$ samples in $d{=}6$ action dimensions). scalar conditioning ($8.5$\% at $\varepsilon{=}0.1$) falls below VLA-only, which confirms that conformal statistics at $K{=}10$ are unreliable regardless of aggregation strategy.

The stability of obs\_only has a direct implication for adaptive $K$ (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k}): observation-only conditioning makes the CRM robust to variable sample counts, a prerequisite for operating across the $K{=}2$ to $K{=}10$ range within a single episode.

\subsection{Distribution Shift Boundaries}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

Three distribution shifts cause the CRM to produce harmful or neutral corrections.

\paragraph{Input shift ($K{=}1$).}
The CRM receives an arbitrary single sample rather than the argmin-centered input assumed during training. Performance drops to 10.3\% (below the 11.3\% baseline), as discussed in Section~\ref{sec:main_results}.

\paragraph{Task shift (proximity-dependent).}
On near-OOD tasks (within-suite leave-task-out), the CRM achieves 2\% vs.\ 14\% VLA-only ($-$12\,pp): it ``recognizes'' the observation context but applies corrections biased toward related training tasks. On far-OOD tasks (cross-suite), the effect depends on distributional distance. Goal-conditioned tasks: $68.0$\% vs.\ $69.6$\% VLA-only ($p{=}0.46$, neutral). Long-horizon tasks: $17.5$\% vs.\ $14.2$\% ($+$3.3\,pp, $p{=}0.046$), where shared manipulation primitives transfer despite the task-level shift. Near-OOD is more dangerous because the CRM confidently applies task-specific biases to distinct but superficially similar tasks.

\paragraph{Feature shift.}
perdof conditioning collapses to 0\% when uncapped, while obs\_only remains stable at 14.3--17.6\% (Section~\ref{sec:magnitude_results}). Noisy per-DOF covariance estimates compound through closed-loop execution~\cite{ross2011dagger}.

All three failure modes share a common cause: the CRM degrades when its input departs from the training distribution. All results in Table~\ref{tab:main} use in-distribution evaluation.

\paragraph{Applicability and overhead.}
The pipeline requires stochastic action diversity: tokenized VLAs~\cite{kim2024openvla} produce near-identical samples (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}). Improvement concentrates on medium-difficulty tasks (Appendix~\ref{sec:per_task}). CRM correction adds $<$0.5\% overhead; batched VLA inference yields ${\sim}$170\,ms for all $K$ (${\sim}$5.5\,Hz; Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}).

